1171875, -0.00677490234375, 0.0017948150634765625, -0.0023822784423828125, 0.00180816650390625, 0.02838134765625, -0.0055389404296875, 0.004802703857421875, -0.009857177734375, 0.0193634033203125, 0.0184173583984375, 0.056549072265625, 0.0015697479248046875, 0.0232086181640625, -0.0357666015625, -0.0259246826171875, 0.02880859375, -0.00725555419921875, 0.0166473388671875, 0.01194000244140625, -0.04266357421875, -0.0203094482421875, 0.00295257568359375, -0.0095062255859375, 0.014373779296875, 0.0211944580078125, -0.01088714599609375, -0.0023670196533203125, 0.005893707275390625, 0.006946563720703125, 0.00261688232421875, 0.0019779205322265625, 0.006298065185546875, -0.01384735107421875, -0.0223388671875, -0.0009293556213378906, -0.0203857421875, 0.007045745849609375, 0.0006203651428222656, -0.00432586669921875, 0.044647216796875, -0.02667236328125, -0.0152587890625, 0.0007510185241699219, 0.01136016845703125, 0.0194091796875, -0.044281005859375, 0.0006628036499023438, 0.0001289844512939453], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where human direction of the task remains. This article asks: at what points in the process does human direction leave observable traces when tasks are delegated to AI? We distinguish two routes. Specified delegation places that direction before execution, through instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task\u2014such as providing a detailed prompt or explicit requirements before the AI begins. Iterative coproduction places it during production, through interventions that correct or redirect provisional outputs\u2014for example, by reviewing and revising AI-generated drafts in real time.\n\nTo examine both routes, we use aggregate monthly cells\u2014summaries of user activity grouped by month and user type\u2014from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI distinguishes two modes of use: 1P API, which corresponds to direct traffic through Anthropic's application programming interface (typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows), and Claude.ai, which combines activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces (used by individuals interacting directly with the AI platform). This distinction is relevant because it reflects different user contexts and interaction patterns.\n\nOn this basis, we test whether a stronger work-related orientation of human-AI interaction is associated with more specified delegation within each mode, and whether the increase in the iterative profile is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. The main analysis uses level-0 O*NET tasks\u2014broad occupational task categories from the O*NET database\u2014to estimate how both profiles change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use. We define node-month pairs as unique combinations of user nodes and months, and restrict the iterative comparison to 1,411 node-month pairs observed and eligible in both modes to ensure comparability and control for confounding differences between user groups.\n\nSpecified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]). In practical terms, this means that as work-related use increases, there is a notable rise in the presence of explicit instructions or constraints, especially in the API context. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). This indicates that iterative, in-process human intervention is more responsive to work-related use in Claude.ai than in the API mode, though the effect size is modest.\n\nThese findings show that work-related orientation is associated with stronger traces of prior human direction and that the observable iterative response varies across modes of use. The article shifts attention from how much the AI executes to when human direction leaves observable traces, highlighting the importance of understanding the timing and form of human input for effective governance and oversight of AI-assisted work."}}